      "id": 1,
      "text": "Canutillo’s story starts in June 1823 when a land grant was given to Juan María Ponce de León and 29 others in what is now Juárez, Chihuahua.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/canutillo-tx"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "A small agricultural settlement appeared in 1824, but Apache raiders forced residents to abandon it in 1833.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/canutillo-tx"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The community sits on the east bank of the Rio Grande, right beside the U.S. border.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/canutillo-tx"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Canutillo was once a stop on the historic Atchison, Topeka and Santa Fe Railway.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
